text:
julia: heads-up from QA—sample-app streaming tests flaky on Pixel; after a token refresh many in-flight streams get 504/stream timeouts. Repro pretty reliably on Android 15.

max: is the sample using a blocking refresh in an interceptor? we've hit that class of issue before.

julia: yes, TokenInterceptor calls refreshBlocking() and it uses the same Retrofit client. snippet: 
```kotlin
override fun intercept(chain: Interceptor.Chain): Response {
  var t = tokenStore.get()
  if (t == null) {
    synchronized(lock) {
      if (tokenStore.get() == null) tokenStore.set(refreshBlocking())
      t = tokenStore.get()
    }
  }
  val r = chain.request().newBuilder().header("Authorization","Bearer $t").build()
  val resp = chain.proceed(r)
  if (resp.code == 401) {
    synchronized(lock) { tokenStore.set(refreshBlocking()) }
    return chain.proceed(r.newBuilder().header("Authorization","Bearer ${tokenStore.get()}").build())
  }
  return resp
}
```

ben: two problems: 1) refreshBlocking via same client will consume dispatcher threads/connection pool -> starvation; 2) the retry-on-401 path allows races where many requests hit 401 and all refresh concurrently.

leo: Android 15's http/2 changes made connection reuse and stream prioritization more aggressive; that amplified the starvation. On older OS this just trailed into slow requests.

max: fixes we used: a) use a dedicated NoAuth OkHttpClient for token endpoint (no interceptors, small dispatcher like maxRequests=2), b) add a singleflight/dedupe so only one refresh happens, c) don't block streaming dispatcher threads (set retryOnConnectionFailure=false for those calls).

julia: implemented NoAuth client + an AtomicReference<CompletableFuture<Token>> singleflight for Java clients. For Kotlin coroutines we used a Mutex + Deferred pattern. Repro disappears locally.

ben: also recommend lowering maxRequestsPerHost on the main dispatcher for mobile (2-4) so one user stream can't starve background refreshes. Add a short README note in sample app about "Do not call same client from interceptor" and show the safe pattern.

leo: one subtlety: if refreshClient reuses same underlying TLS config and the platform aggressively reuses connections, still watch pool sizes. Best to set ConnectionPool small for refresh client and explicit timeouts.

julia: I'll open a patch to sample app: replace blocking refresh with dedupe + refreshClient, add integration test toggling airplane mode to simulate token storms, and update onboarding docs.

ci-bot: build/24178 started for julia/token-refresh-sample-fix; running unit + integration tests.

julia: thanks  pinging when PR is up for review.
